# Contributing

<!-- Per-repo. Fill in the install/test/lint commands below. Rename to
     CONTRIBUTING.md (drop .example). Delete this comment before
     committing a filled copy. -->

## Development Setup

- Install dependencies: `<install command>`
- Run the test suite: `<test command>`
- Run lint checks: `<lint command>`

## Conventions

- Branch names: `<type>/<kebab-description>` (feat, fix, chore, docs,
  test).
- Commit subjects: `type: description`, imperative mood, 50 characters
  max, no trailing period.

## Safety

- Use parameterized queries for untrusted input.
- Run subprocesses with argument arrays and no shell interpretation.
- Get explicit approval before destructive changes.
- Never weaken, skip, or delete a test to force a pass. If a test looks
  wrong, stop and ask a maintainer before changing it.
- Keep public APIs backward compatible.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, or private user data.
- Use SHA-256 or SHA-3 for general hashing.
- Use bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 with a salt and work factor for passwords.
- Run containers as a non-root user at runtime.
- Set `actions/checkout` `persist-credentials: false` unless the job later
  pushes, uses the checked-out credential through Git, or fetches private
  submodules or LFS objects. Document an exception with
  `# persist-credentials: true: this job <reason> (Rule 11 exception).`
- Add commits instead of rewriting shared branch history.

## Dependencies

- Before changing a dependency, propose its name, pinned version, purpose,
  and alternatives for maintainer approval.
- Pin approved dependencies to exact versions.

## Change Workflow

1. Create a branch following the naming convention above.
2. Add a test that exercises the real behavior, then run it to confirm it
   fails for the expected reason.
3. Implement the smallest change that makes the new test pass.
4. Run `<test command>` and `<lint command>`. Fix failures without
   suppressing checks.
5. Update README for substantial changes and CHANGELOG for all changes.
   Follow Semantic Versioning.
6. Commit using the subject convention above.
7. Open every pull request as a draft with the change, risks, and
   verification results.
